Gateway backup inventory availability and confirmation safety
============================================================

The gateway backup tab treats an unavailable inventory differently from a
successfully retrieved empty inventory. It validates the response gateway ID,
vendor field, count, unique safe filenames, timestamps, integer byte sizes and
descriptions before displaying metadata. It does not infer that no backups
exist from a failed request or malformed response.

While an inventory request or refresh is pending, old rows and their restore
and delete controls are hidden. Failed refreshes do not expose cached rows.
An explicit retry reads only the inventory. Configuration export remains a
separate action and does not depend on backup history being available.

Restore and delete confirmations are tied to the displayed inventory snapshot.
A refresh, failed read or changed snapshot invalidates an open confirmation.
An old confirmation does not reopen when a refresh returns identical metadata.
File sizes use size_bytes, including zero. English, Spanish and Chinese text
distinguishes loading, unavailable and retry states.

Validation and limits
---------------------
Rendered page regression tests cover failed/malformed results, valid empty
history, byte sizes, retries, stale rows, confirmation invalidation and normal
confirmed dispatch. They use the real page, query cache, table and dialogs with
controlled API functions. Additional Chromium checks use the actual page and
Axios over owned loopback HTTP with synthetic API responses, including invalid
array/row shapes and successful confirmation dispatch to a synthetic receiver.
These are explicitly not tests of real authentication or hardware restoration.

This change is a browser safety guard, not a server-side inventory lock or
proof that a filename still names the same artifact when a device processes a
write. It cannot prevent a device changing independently after an inventory
read. Backend permissions and write policy remain necessary.

Managed gateway backup artifact storage, retention and restore qualification
remain incomplete. OPNsense create/delete/revert compatibility still requires
separate protocol review and authorized disposable-device write tests.
OpenWRT's existing inventory behavior is unchanged here. This change does not
qualify those operations, provide RPO/RTO measurements, or establish enterprise
release readiness. See GATEWAY-BACKUP-INVENTORY and GATEWAY-BACKUP-TRIGGER.
